CONTEXT: MyTripPlanner-based "AI Smart Trip Planner" (React 19 + Vite + zustand + Leaflet frontend, Node server in /server, free rule-based agent in server/freeAgent.mjs). India support already works (scored matcher in server/destination.mjs, Trie autocomplete, tests). Do NOT change India behavior. Goal: robust trip search and planning for ANY destination worldwide using free, keyless services. Ignore any earlier "worldwide add-on" instructions; this replaces them.

STEP 0 - STATE CHECK (brief): run `git status`, `git log --oneline -5`, `npm test`. Print DONE / PARTIAL / TODO per item, work only on non-DONE items. Commit after every 2-3 items. If budget is almost gone, commit and print "STOPPED AT: item N, remaining: ...". Short replies: one line per change, never re-print whole files.

1. SERVER-SIDE PLACE SERVICE (new server/worldPlaces.mjs, exposed via new API routes)
   - All Nominatim, Wikipedia and Overpass calls go through the Node server, never directly from the browser (Nominatim requires a real User-Agent, e.g. "MyTripPlanner/1.0 (contact: <email from env NOMINATIM_EMAIL>)"; browsers cannot set it).
   - Global rate limiter: max 1 Nominatim request/second (queue), 10s timeout, 1 retry with backoff, AbortController support.
   - In-memory LRU cache (with TTL 24h) keyed by the normalized query; also cache Wikipedia/Overpass results per destination. Never cache failures.
   - Add an env switch WORLD_PLACES_ENABLED (default true) and document it.

2. RESOLUTION ORDER for a destination text
   a. India first: findDestination (existing scored matcher). If it matches, use the existing flow unchanged.
   b. Otherwise geocode worldwide: Nominatim search, format=jsonv2, addressdetails=1, limit=5, accept-language=en, no countrycodes filter. Normalize the query (trim, collapse spaces, strip "trip to / plan / visit / days in / itinerary" filler words, extract the place name and any trip length like "5 days").
   c. Accept a candidate only if its name/display_name contains the searched text (whole-word, accent-insensitive). Otherwise ask "Which destination did you mean?".
   d. DISAMBIGUATION: if 2+ candidates are plausible with similar importance (e.g. Paris, France vs Paris, Texas; Georgia the country vs the US state), do NOT guess. Ask the user to pick from a short list showing display_name (city, region, country). If one candidate clearly dominates (importance gap), use it.
   e. Classify the result by type/class: city/town/village/island -> plan directly. country/state/region/continent -> do NOT plan around one point; ask "Which city in <X>?" with 3-5 suggestions (bundled list of top cities per ~40 popular countries, e.g. Japan -> Tokyo, Kyoto, Osaka), unless the user names several cities (then build a multi-city trip, splitting days per city).
   f. Also handle typos and native spellings via Nominatim's results; if nothing found, say so honestly and suggest checking spelling. Never fall back to a default destination and never invent places.

3. ATTRACTIONS, FREE AND KEYLESS
   - Wikipedia geosearch around the destination centre: action=query&list=geosearch&gscoord=LAT|LNG&gsradius=10000&gslimit=50&format=json (radius max is 10000 m; for big cities also sample 3-4 points around the bounding box to widen coverage). Then fetch short extracts (prop=extracts|pageimages|coordinates, exintro, explaintext, exsentences=2) in batches of up to 20 titles.
   - Filter and rank: drop non-tourist pages (disambiguation, streets, schools, hospitals, stations unless famous, districts/neighbourhood pages), dedupe near-duplicates, prefer pages with longer extracts and popular categories (museums, monuments, parks, temples, palaces, viewpoints, markets, beaches).
   - Each POI needs a real name, real lat/lng, and a one-to-two sentence description from Wikipedia (attribute Wikipedia). No generic filler text.
   - Fallback chain if Wikipedia fails or returns fewer than 6 usable POIs: (1) Overpass API (OpenStreetMap, keyless) query for tourism=attraction/museum/viewpoint within 8 km, limit 40; (2) a bundled offline list for ~40 popular cities (Paris, London, Rome, Barcelona, Amsterdam, Istanbul, Dubai, Abu Dhabi, Singapore, Bangkok, Phuket, Bali/Denpasar, Kuala Lumpur, Tokyo, Kyoto, Osaka, Seoul, Hong Kong, Sydney, Melbourne, New York, Los Angeles, San Francisco, Las Vegas, Toronto, Vancouver, Cairo, Cape Town, Marrakech, Prague, Vienna, Berlin, Lisbon, Athens, Maldives/Male, Kathmandu, Colombo, Bali, Hanoi, Ho Chi Minh City) with 6+ attractions each and real coordinates; (3) if all fail, tell the user attraction data is unavailable right now and do NOT fabricate a plan.

4. ITINERARY BUILDER for worldwide trips (reuse the India day-builder)
   - 3-4 stops per day at staggered times (09:30, 12:30, 16:00, 19:00), trip length from the query (default 3-5 days).
   - Geographic clustering so each day is compact: cluster POIs (k-means or grid clustering) into N days, order each day's stops with nearest-neighbour + 2-opt, and start each day from the previous day's last cluster. Implement these as small tested pure functions (great for explaining in my DSA viva) with Haversine distance.
   - Day titles/themes derived from the actual stops (e.g. "Old Town & Museums"), never generic mismatches.
   - Estimated travel time between stops via OSRM if reachable, otherwise Haversine at a stated average speed.

5. CURRENCY, LOCALE, UNITS
   - Map country_code -> currency for ~60 common countries (FR/DE/IT/ES/NL/PT/GR/AT -> EUR, US -> USD, GB -> GBP, CH -> CHF, AE -> AED, TR -> TRY, JP -> JPY, KR -> KRW, CN -> CNY, HK -> HKD, TH -> THB, ID -> IDR, MY -> MYR, SG -> SGD, VN -> VND, AU -> AUD, NZ -> NZD, CA -> CAD, MX -> MXN, ZA -> ZAR, EG -> EGP, MA -> MAD, NP -> NPR, LK -> LKR, MV -> MVR, etc.). Trip currency = destination's currency; INR only for India. Show the right symbol everywhere and give a rough default budget per day in that currency (bundled per-country tier table, clearly labeled "estimate").
   - Show distances in km, and local time zone of the destination where times are displayed.

6. HOTELS AND RESTAURANTS (be honest)
   - Use the existing provider interface. If a provider returns real data, use it; otherwise use Overpass (tourism=hotel/hostel/guest_house, amenity=restaurant/cafe) to list real places near the day's stops WITHOUT invented prices or ratings (show "price not available").
   - Mock providers must use the destination's real coordinates and currency. The agent's final message must only claim what was actually added.

7. MAP AND UI
   - Map tiles: use the shared constant (OpenStreetMap by default, override via VITE_MAP_TILE_URL / VITE_MAP_TILE_ATTRIBUTION); no CARTO key needed. Only restrict searches to India (countrycodes=in) when the user is in the India flow.
   - Fit map bounds to the destination boundingbox and to the stops; show every day's markers/route in its color.
   - Search box: keep the Trie autocomplete for India names, and add debounced (400 ms) worldwide suggestions from the server route, with cancel-on-new-keystroke and a "No results for 'X'" state.
   - Title-case the trip name ("paris" -> "Paris"); page title matches.
   - Handle non-Latin input (e.g. "東京", "Москва"), extra spaces, mixed case, and emoji/garbage gracefully.
   - Loading, error and empty states everywhere; no unhandled promise rejections; agent never stays stuck loading.

8. OPTIONAL AI KEY (do not break the free agent)
   - The free agent must work with NO key. If an AI provider key is configured (via Admin/env), the LLM may improve wording only; the POIs, coordinates and currency must still come from the services above. A missing or invalid key falls back to the free agent with a friendly message. Keys stay server-side; .env.example lists variables; .env is git-ignored.

9. TESTS (Vitest; mock fetch and NEVER call real APIs in tests)
   Cases: Paris (dominant result vs Paris, Texas disambiguation), Dubai, Bali, Tokyo, New York, London, "Japan" (country -> asks which city), "Kyoto and Osaka" (multi-city), "東京", typos ("Pariss"), gibberish and "fort tour" (asks which destination), Goa/Himachal/Old Manali still resolve to India correctly, Nominatim rate limiter and cache, Wikipedia failure -> Overpass -> bundled fallback -> honest "unavailable" message, POI filtering/dedupe, clustering + nearest-neighbour + 2-opt correctness (close stops share a day, total distance never worse than input order), Haversine, currency mapping, budget in local currency, honest agent message when hotels/restaurants are empty, title-casing.

